## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about Kubernetes resources and Spark on Kubernetes, and perform practical memory tuning with metrics, GC, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes resource requests/limits
- Spark memory tuning (driver/executor memory, memoryOverhead, spark.memory.fraction)
- JVM GC options and GC logging
- Shuffle-heavy workloads and GC pressure
- Observability (Spark UI, kubectl top, metrics)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kubernetes patch example (pseudo)
const patch = {
  spec: {
    template: {
      spec: {
        containers: [
          { name: "executor",
            resources: { requests: { memory: "12Gi" }, limits: { memory: "12Gi" } }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect a memory leak vs normal GC pauses?
- How would you verify stability after changes with a canary run?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the candidate’s ability to design a production-grade Kubernetes operator handling data ingestion with strong consistency, reliability, and cross-region considerations. It also touches Snowflake integration, secret management, and robust testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes operators and CRDs
- Reconciliation loops and single-writer pattern
- Idempotency and exactly-once semantics
- Finalizers and cleanup
- Backoff and error handling
- Observability with metrics and events
- Security: secret management and RBAC
- Testing: fault injection and end-to-end replay

